  "intro": {
    "language": "en",
    "text": "Mate Rimac (Croatian: [mǎːte rǐːmats]; born 12 February 1988) is an entrepreneur and engineer. He is the founder and CEO of the Rimac Group, a business which includes Bugatti Rimac – composed of the Bugatti Automobiles and Rimac Automobili brands – and Rimac Technology, a supplier of technology to automotive brands. He also founded Greyp Bikes, a high-tech eBike and eBike technology company which was taken over by Porsche AG and is now known as Porsche eBike Performance GmbH. According to Nedeljnik, as of December 2025, Rimac's estimated net worth was €1.74 billion, making him the second-richest individual in Croatia.\nIn his high school years, Rimac won local, national, and international competitions for electronics and innovation. At 19 years old, he started to convert a 1984 BMW 3 Series into an electric car in his garage. The vehicle broke several world records for electric cars. At the age of 23 in 2011, Rimac went on to create his first all-electric supercar, the Concept One.\nHis company, Rimac Automobili, went from its first employees in 2011 to more than 1,000 employees in 2020, with major investment from Porsche AG, Hyundai-Kia, and Camel Group. In addition to developing and...",
    "sourceId": "wikipedia-en",
    "usage": "Research excerpt; paraphrase and cross-check before publication."
  },
